@page "/components/ai/toolresultview"

<PageOutlet Url="components/ai/toolresultview"
            Title="AiToolResultView"
            Description="BitAiToolResultView shows a tool result carried as a message part of its own - as text, as a JSON tree, or as a compact error." />

<DemoPage Name="AiToolResultView"
          Description="BitAiToolResultView shows a tool result carried on its own (a BitAiToolResultPart) rather than on the call it answers - a conversation imported from a store that keeps each result as a message of its own: a card headed by the tool's name, with the result as it was written, as a JSON tree when it is structured, or as a compact error when it failed. A result streamed in by a transport always goes to its call and is shown by the BitAiToolCallView of that call."
          Parameters="componentParameters"
          SubClasses="componentSubClasses"
          CssVariables="componentCssVariables"
          GitHubAIUrl="ToolResultView/BitAiToolResultView.razor"
          GitHubDemoUrl="AI/ToolResultView/BitAiToolResultViewDemo.razor">
    <NotesTemplate>
        <BitText>
            To use this component, you need to install the
            <BitLink Href="https://www.nuget.org/packages/Bit.BlazorUI.AI" Target="_blank">
                <BitTag Reversed
                        Text="Bit.BlazorUI.AI"
                        Color="BitColor.SecondaryBackground"
                        IconName="@BitIconName.NavigateExternalInline" />
            </BitLink>
            nuget package, as described in the Optional steps of the
            <BitLink Href="/getting-started">Getting started</BitLink> page.
        </BitText>
        <br />
        <BitText>
            A tool result a transport streams in always goes to the <BitLink Href="/components/ai/toolcallview">BitAiToolCallView</BitLink>
            of its call, matched by <b>ToolCallId</b>: the stream processor holds a result that arrives ahead of its call until the call starts,
            and drops one whose call never does (<b>BitAiChatSessionOptions.ToolResultHoldTime</b>, ten seconds by default) with an error part on
            the message. This view is for a result carried on its own - a part of the <b>tool-result</b> kind an app adds itself, for a
            conversation imported from a store that keeps each result as a message of its own - and <b>BitAiMessagePartView</b> renders it
            for such a part. The result is the tool's: it is shown as text and as a JSON tree, never as markup. See the
            <BitLink Href="/ai/scenarios/tools">Tools</BitLink> scenario.
        </BitText>
    </NotesTemplate>
    <Examples>
        <DemoExample Title="Basic" RazorCode="@example1RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example1CsharpCode" Id="example1">
            <div>
                Hand it the <b>Part</b> (a <b>BitAiToolResultPart</b>). The card is headed by the <b>ToolName</b> of the part, as it is written
                (no tool definition is read for it), under the wrench of a tool; a result that carries <b>Text</b> is shown as it was written - its
                line breaks kept, a long token wrapped. <b>Text</b> wins over a <b>Value</b> the outcome carries as well.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-stack">
                <BitAiToolResultView Part="textResult" />
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Structured result" RazorCode="@example2RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example2CsharpCode" Id="example2">
            <div>
                A result that carries only a <b>Value</b> is shown as a <BitLink Href="/components/ai/jsonview">BitAiJsonView</BitLink> tree.
                Without a <b>ToolName</b>, the card is headed <b>Result</b> (the <b>BitAiStrings.Result</b> of the options in force).
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-stack">
                <BitAiToolResultView Part="jsonResult" />
                <BitAiToolResultView Part="unnamedResult" />
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Failure" RazorCode="@example3RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example3CsharpCode" Id="example3">
            <div>
                A failed result (<b>BitAiToolOutcome.Failure</b>) is shown as a compact
                <BitLink Href="/components/ai/errorview">BitAiErrorView</BitLink>, titled by its message, with its detail behind a disclosure.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-stack">
                <BitAiToolResultView Part="failedResult" />
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="In a message" RazorCode="@example4RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example4CsharpCode" Id="example4">
            <div>
                In a conversation the part is one of the parts of an assistant message, and
                <BitLink Href="/components/ai/messagepartview">BitAiMessagePartView</BitLink> renders it with this view, like any other part.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-stack">
                @foreach (var part in resultMessage.Parts)
                {
                    <BitAiMessagePartView @key="part.PartId" Part="part" Message="resultMessage" />
                }
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Cascading parameters" RazorCode="@example5RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example5CsharpCode" Id="example5">
            <div>
                <b>BitParams</b> with a <b>BitAiToolResultViewParams</b> sets the defaults of every result view inside it - here a dashed outline
                and an italic title - the ones a <b>BitAiMessagePartView</b> renders included. It is a default, not an override: a view keeps what
                it sets itself (the last one brings its own <b>Styles</b>). The part itself is never cascaded.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-stack">
                <BitParams Parameters="resultViewParams">
                    <BitAiToolResultView Part="textResult" />
                    <BitAiToolResultView Part="failedResult" />
                    <BitAiToolResultView Part="jsonResult" Styles="@(new() { Root = "border-style: solid", Title = "font-weight: bold" })" />
                </BitParams>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Style & Class" RazorCode="@example6RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example6CsharpCode" Id="example6">
            <div>
                <b>Style</b> and <b>Class</b> land on the root, <b>Styles</b> and <b>Classes</b> on the header, its icon and title, the body and
                a textual result. The public <b>--bit-AiToolResultView-*</b> variables (see the CSS variables table) inherit, so a container
                restyles every card under it.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-stack">
                <BitAiToolResultView Part="textResult" Style="box-shadow: 0 0.25rem 1rem #0003" />

                <BitAiToolResultView Part="textResult" Class="custom-result" />

                <BitAiToolResultView Part="textResult"
                                     Styles="@(new() { Icon = "color: #0b7a75",
                                                       Title = "font-style: italic",
                                                       Text = "font-family: inherit" })" />

                <BitAiToolResultView Part="textResult"
                                     Classes="@(new() { Header = "custom-result-header",
                                                        Body = "custom-result-body" })" />

                <div style="--bit-AiToolResultView-background: var(--bit-clr-suc-tint); --bit-AiToolResultView-border-color: var(--bit-clr-suc); --bit-AiToolResultView-header-color: var(--bit-clr-suc-fg); --bit-AiToolResultView-body-background: transparent; --bit-AiToolResultView-radius: 1rem;">
                    <BitAiToolResultView Part="jsonResult" />
                </div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="RTL" RazorCode="@example7RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example7CsharpCode" Id="example7">
            <div>
                In right-to-left text the card is laid out from the right - the icon leads the title on the right, and the result reads from it -
                whether the direction comes from <b>Dir</b> or from a <b>dir</b> attribute around the card. The heading of a result with no
                <b>ToolName</b> is the <b>Result</b> text of the <b>BitAiStrings</b> in force - here a subclass of them, cascaded by a
                <b>BitAiOptionsProvider</b>.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-stack">
                <BitAiToolResultView Dir="BitDir.Rtl" Part="rtlResult" />
                <BitAiOptionsProvider UiOptions="persianOptions">
                    <div dir="rtl">
                        <BitAiToolResultView Part="rtlUnnamedResult" />
                    </div>
                </BitAiOptionsProvider>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>
    </Examples>
</DemoPage>
